; STORE-MODEL Q13 / amendment A-6, landed with the F-26 clause in window A
; (RULINGS W3-10, adjudicated 2026-08-25): `canonS` recurses into `lit` payloads —
; one carrier, one canonical form.
;
; FLIPPED 2026-08-25 by W3-12 + W3-18 — the amendment record PROCEDURE §5 asks for.
;
; What this fixture asserted until today: two `lit` schemas whose OBJECT payloads differ
; only in key order take the same address, so A-6's dedup extends into the lit plane
; (F-23 closed). Both puts exited 0 and `(assert-same @1 @2)` held.
;
; What it asserts now: those two schemas are INADMISSIBLE and never reach the store.
; W3-18 made `litNarrowB` a `WFS` conjunct (MAPPING admission rule 1, made decidable,
; F-35), and W3-12 wired the boundary to the model's admission verdict. A `.lit` payload
; is a string, a boolean, or an integer and nothing else, so `(lit (obj …))` is rejected
; `not-well-formed lit-narrow` before canonicity is ever consulted (W3-13's order).
;
; A-6 is NOT retracted by this. `canonS` still recurses into `lit` payloads and the
; kernel receipts still hold; what changed is that the recursion has no OBSERVABLE at the
; boundary any more, because every admissible payload is a scalar with no internal order
; to canonicalize. The dedup claim moved from "provable at the shell" to "true and
; unreachable", and the honest form of this fixture is the rejection below.

; the two A-6 shapes, now inadmissible on the payload-domain clause
(schema-put (lit (obj ("alpha" (i 1)) ("beta" (s "x")))))
(assert-code prev 1)
(schema-put (lit (obj ("beta" (s "x")) ("alpha" (i 1)))))
(assert-code prev 1)

; the payload domain that IS admitted (MAPPING admission rule 1): str, bool, int
(schema-put (lit (s "x")))
(assert-code prev 0)
(schema-put (lit true))
(assert-code prev 0)
(schema-put (lit (i 7)))
(assert-code prev 0)

; and the same literal twice is one object, not two — L-dedup, on the surviving domain
(schema-put (lit (s "x")))
(assert-same @5 @11)

; nothing rejected ever landed: three objects, not five.
(check)
